<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alceste De Ambris</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1874">1874</a>–<a href="/years/1934">1934</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alceste De Ambris was an Italian revolutionary syndicalist and trade union organiser. He campaigned for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>'s entry into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and in 1920 drafted with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabriele-dannunzio">Gabriele D'Annunzio</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charter-of-carnaro">Charter of Carnaro</a>, the constitution of the regency at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rijeka">Fiume</a>. He later opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascism">Fascism</a> and died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alceste-de-ambris.jpg" alt="Alceste De Ambris" width="320" height="418" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alceste_de_Ambris_1930.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>De Ambris was born on 15 September 1874 in Licciana Nardi, in the province of Massa-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carrara">Carrara</a>, the first of eight children of Francesco De Ambris, a Mazzinian republican, and Valeria Ricci. Moved by the hard lives of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lunigiana">Lunigiana</a> workers, he joined the Italian Socialist Party in 1892 and helped found socialist circles at Aulla and La Spezia. He studied law at the University of Parma from 1893 to 1895 but left without a degree, took part in protests against the colonial wars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and in 1897 tried to desert from military service to fight for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-turkish-war-1897">Greco-Turkish War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He then went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, where he studied the conditions of Italian immigrants on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> plantations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a> and helped them form associations and unions. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> he became secretary of the Chamber of Labor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savona">Savona</a> and, at the end of 1904, of the National Glassmakers' Federation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livorno">Livorno</a>. After the repression that followed the Parma strike of 1908 he left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and returned to Brazil, where he edited La Tribuna Italiana for ten months and in 1910 founded his own paper, La Scure.</p><p>In 1912 he opposed the syndicalists who backed the Libyan War, above all Arturo Labriola, and became one of the leaders of the Unione Sindacale Italiana, founded that November with 80,000 workers. He worked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and briefly afterwards but never joined the Fasci di Combattimento. He sided with the Arditi del Popolo, took part in the fighting between the workers of Parma and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italo-balbo">Italo Balbo</a>'s squads in August 1922, and after being beaten in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> that December went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. There he refused Mussolini's approaches, worked as a bookseller and joined the Lega Italiana dei Diritti dell'Uomo with Luigi Campolonghi. He lost his Italian citizenship in 1926.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>In articles for Divenire Sociale, La Gioventù Socialista and Il Sindacato Operaio, De Ambris argued that the trade union was the instrument that would transform society and let the workers manage power, and that unions should be independent of political parties. He accused parliamentary socialism, which had worked with the governments of Giuseppe Zanardelli and Giovanni Giolitti since 1901, of sinking into legalism. In exile he founded the weekly Il Mezzogiorno in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> and in 1926 wrote a pamphlet on the murder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giacomo-matteotti">Giacomo Matteotti</a> that held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> responsible. His books include La questione di <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rijeka">Fiume</a> (1920) and a profile of Filippo Corridoni (1922).</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alceste De Ambris” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the First World War began, most syndicalists opposed it, but the majority of the CGT and a minority of the USI, led by <b>Alceste De Ambris</b>, supported their countries.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/syndicalism">Syndicalism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>Its constitution, the Charter of Carnaro, drafted with the syndicalist <b>Alceste De Ambris</b>, organised citizens into corporations and declared music a fundamental institution of the state.</blockquote><a href="/state/italian-regency-of-carnaro">Italian Regency of Carnaro</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Licciana Nardi, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brive-la-gaillarde">Brive-la-Gaillarde</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.27,10.03&amp;year=1913" aria-label="Licciana Nardi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Alceste De Ambris"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5611 3766h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5611 3766h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.27,10.03&amp;year=1913">What was here in 1913? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
